WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.059
Module 4: settings, privacy and testing.

00:00:03.059 --> 00:00:09.406
Settings decide what respondents experience
and what you can honestly promise them.

00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:13.842
Testing shows the survey works
before anyone real sees it.

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:15.830
About thirty-five minutes.

00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:18.918
Display options shape the experience.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:22.690
A progress bar and question
numbers are on by default.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.951
One question per page works well
on phones for short surveys.

00:00:26.951 --> 00:00:30.164
Allow back navigation
lets people fix answers.

00:00:30.164 --> 00:00:33.517
And leave shuffle blocks
off when order matters.

00:00:33.517 --> 00:00:35.194
Then save your settings.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:41.417
Anonymous Responses is on by default, and
it's worth knowing exactly what it does.

00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:45.948
SurveyCraft doesn't store anyone's
IP address, browser or device.

00:00:45.948 --> 00:00:51.595
A response that came through someone's
personal link isn't linked to their roster

00:00:51.595 --> 00:00:52.013
entry.

00:00:52.013 --> 00:00:56.196
The roster's viewed and completed
times are kept to the day,

00:00:56.196 --> 00:01:00.449
so nobody can match a person to
a response by the exact time.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:04.492
And respondents see a line
saying the survey is anonymous.

00:01:04.492 --> 00:01:10.278
Turn it off, and SurveyCraft stores IP,
browser and device, and the footer says so.

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:14.949
With personal links, every answer
is then linked to a named person.

00:01:14.949 --> 00:01:18.435
Remember the survey you
thought about in Module 0?

00:01:18.435 --> 00:01:20.317
Now you know what to check.

00:01:20.833 --> 00:01:22.676
So which should you choose?

00:01:22.676 --> 00:01:27.181
Anonymous suits culture and climate
pulses and anything sensitive.

00:01:27.181 --> 00:01:30.389
You can still see who
has finished, to the day,

00:01:30.389 --> 00:01:32.915
and roster demographics still attach.

00:01:32.915 --> 00:01:38.375
Identified suits follow-up you've agreed
in advance, like a training needs list.

00:01:38.375 --> 00:01:44.108
Decide before launch, say the same thing
everywhere, and don't switch it mid-survey.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.416
Anonymous Responses removes
identity from the record.

00:01:48.416 --> 00:01:51.657
It can't stop identification
by other routes.

00:01:51.657 --> 00:01:56.121
A small group, like four clinic
managers, is as good as named.

00:01:56.121 --> 00:02:01.449
People write their own names and stories
into open text, and attach files.

00:02:01.449 --> 00:02:07.354
Analytics hides groups below the minimum
group size, but exports are the raw rows,

00:02:07.354 --> 00:02:11.170
and a group of three or four
can still be recognized.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.987
And everyone in the workspace
can read the responses.

00:02:14.987 --> 00:02:18.443
So promise "anonymous,
results shared by group".

00:02:18.443 --> 00:02:20.675
Never "impossible to identify".

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:24.706
Settings has a switch called
prevent ballot stuffing.

00:02:24.706 --> 00:02:26.508
Here's what really happens.

00:02:26.508 --> 00:02:30.648
Once a browser submits, it can't
submit the same survey again.

00:02:30.648 --> 00:02:32.985
Nothing about the person is stored,

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:38.060
but someone on a second phone or in a
private window can still answer twice.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.335
On an anonymous survey that's the only
check, because addresses are never kept.

00:02:43.335 --> 00:02:48.610
On an identified survey, SurveyCraft can
also refuse a second response from the

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:54.085
same network address, and colleagues on
one hospital network can share an address,

00:02:54.085 --> 00:02:57.424
so a nurse who hasn't
answered may be turned away.

00:02:57.424 --> 00:03:00.228
On a shared kiosk, switch the setting off.

00:03:00.228 --> 00:03:02.699
What always works is a personal link.

00:03:02.699 --> 00:03:05.703
A roster or email
invitation link works once.

00:03:05.703 --> 00:03:09.710
So when one response per person
matters, use personal links.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.718
Use a shared link or QR code where you
have no email, and watch the counts.

00:03:15.233 --> 00:03:19.420
Set a close date, and the link
stops taking answers then,

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.606
though the status still reads
Live until you press Close.

00:03:23.606 --> 00:03:28.454
A maximum number of responses turns
people away once it's reached.

00:03:28.454 --> 00:03:34.329
Test responses don't count toward it, but
leave it empty for most pulses anyway.

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:38.883
Save and continue lets people
finish later on the same device.

00:03:38.883 --> 00:03:44.979
And the data retention period deletes
responses a set time after they're completed.

00:03:44.979 --> 00:03:46.815
Agree it with the client.

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:52.534
If you need a file from respondents,
SurveyCraft accepts images, PDFs,

00:03:52.534 --> 00:03:55.226
CSV and text files, and Excel files.

00:03:55.226 --> 00:03:58.142
Not Word documents, and not SVG images.

00:03:58.142 --> 00:04:02.927
Each file can be up to ten megabytes,
with up to five at a time.

00:04:02.927 --> 00:04:06.292
The question's settings
stop at those limits.

00:04:06.292 --> 00:04:10.030
Only members of your
workspace can open the files.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:15.788
And remember the privacy cost: a photo
or document can identify someone in an

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.059
anonymous survey.

00:04:17.059 --> 00:04:20.423
Ask for files only when
you really need them.

00:04:20.933 --> 00:04:22.780
There are two testing tools.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:28.055
Preview shows the survey on a computer,
a tablet and a phone, and saves nothing.

00:04:28.055 --> 00:04:31.352
Use it to read every question
and walk every path.

00:04:31.352 --> 00:04:36.825
Test responses create a batch of made-up
answers while the survey is still a draft.

00:04:36.825 --> 00:04:42.034
They follow your logic, so their rows on
the Responses tab show whether routing

00:04:42.034 --> 00:04:42.429
works.

00:04:42.429 --> 00:04:46.056
They're left out of your
charts and any response limit,

00:04:46.056 --> 00:04:50.870
but they stay on the Responses list
and in exports until you delete them.

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:52.731
So here's the rule.

00:04:52.731 --> 00:04:57.757
Before you publish, go to the Responses
tab and delete test responses.

00:04:57.757 --> 00:05:00.844
Then check the Responses
tab shows no rows.

00:05:00.844 --> 00:05:04.506
Test rows in a client's
export are a serious error.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.074
Here's a checklist that pulls it all
together, from the brief to the roster.

00:05:10.074 --> 00:05:13.411
Download it and use it on
every survey you launch.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.521
Now set up the huddle pulse for launch.

00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:20.015
Six decisions, with Riley,
Sam and Alex weighing in.

00:05:20.015 --> 00:05:25.054
Watch for critical errors: any one ends
the attempt, and you can try again.

00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:27.656
Quick check before Module 5.

00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:28.970
To sum up.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:34.754
Anonymity removes identity from the
record, but small groups, open text,

00:05:34.754 --> 00:05:38.289
filters and exports can
still expose people.

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:41.020
Promise what the settings deliver.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:44.314
Use personal links for one response each.

00:05:44.314 --> 00:05:47.688
And test, then delete your test responses.

00:05:47.688 --> 00:05:51.625
Next, Module 5: fielding,
analysis and reporting.

